+Why Track B.  A polynomial method exists for this planted distribution.  Two
+teams are in the same latent tree-metric cluster exactly when their distance
+rows differ by one constant outside those two columns.  The reference algorithm
+checks every pair against every remaining column, then derives the functional
+normal to a recovered triplet.  At shipping n=126 this is exactly 976,500
+row-difference subtractions and succeeds on every tested seed.  It is reported
+as reference_algorithm, never disguised as a failed Track-A attack.  The short
+route instead sums the public tags: construction makes the sum vector
+orthogonal to the planted functional, so swapping and negating its coordinates
+gives a valid certificate in at most 255 exact arithmetic operations.
 
+Inverse construction.  Sample distinct nonzero coefficients a,b with
+a != +/-b first.  Sample distinct fiber values whose sum is zero, and draw three
+distinct points on each affine line a*u+b*v=value.  Consequently the total tag
+sum is orthogonal to (a,b).  Attach the three points of each fiber to a hidden
+tree-metric center.  A true triplet perimeter has no center-to-root edge; every
+mixed triplet has positive extra cost.  Thus the planted fibers uniquely
+minimize the Section 5 road-trip score, while every nonzero scalar multiple of
+(a,b) is an accepted symbolic witness.  No completed instance is solved.
 
