 NOTES = r"""
-Paper definition and Step 0.  Section 2 fixes the native BTTP constraints:
-each cross-league pair plays once at each venue in 2n slots; no home stand or
-road trip exceeds three games; consecutive games cannot repeat an opponent;
-teams leave from and return to home.  Theorem 1 proves BTTP and uniform BTTP*
-NP-complete through a 3-SAT gadget, but worst-case NP-completeness says nothing
-about this generator's distribution.  Section 4 is the easy warning: for n=6,
-Propositions 1 and 2 reduce and propagate individual schedules, solving the NPB
-instance in 34,716 seconds.  Section 5 is the construction used here: partition
-both leagues into triplets and pair triplets in three-slot Latin blocks to get a
-uniform feasible tournament.  For n=15 it enumerates 15!/(5!*2^5) =
-340,540,200 non-equivalent ordered road-trip plans in minutes.
+Paper definition and Step 0. Section 2 defines BTTP travel: a team begins at
+home, travels directly between consecutive away venues, and returns home after
+its last away game. The at-most-three constraint limits a road trip to three
+games. Section 5 then computes an individual lower bound ILB_t by partitioning
+the opposing league into three-team trips and enumerating
+n!/((n/3)!*2^(n/3)) ordered trip plans. That is the native problem generated
+here; no graph, finite-field, or SAT surrogate replaces it.
 
-Why Track B.  A polynomial method exists for this planted distribution.  Two
-teams are in the same latent tree-metric cluster exactly when their distance
-rows differ by one constant outside those two columns.  The reference algorithm
-checks every pair against every remaining column, then derives the functional
-normal to a recovered triplet.  At shipping n=126 this is exactly 976,500
-row-difference subtractions and succeeds on every tested seed.  It is reported
-as reference_algorithm, never disguised as a failed Track-A attack.  The short
-route instead sums the public tags: construction makes the sum vector
-orthogonal to the planted functional, so swapping and negating its coordinates
-gives a valid certificate in at most 255 exact arithmetic operations.
+What is hard and what is easy. Theorem 1 proves general BTTP and uniform BTTP*
+NP-complete, but that worst-case result does not establish hardness for an
+inverse-generated distribution. Section 4's Propositions 1 and 2 are the easy
+warning: global constraints and individual lower bounds prune the special
+n=6 NPB instance, although the reported computation still took 34,716 seconds.
+This module therefore makes the narrower Track-B claim. Its completed tree
+metrics have a polynomial reconstruction algorithm: the home slack
+D(H,i)+D(H,j)-D(i,j) is positive exactly for sibling leaves. The shipping scan
+uses C(126,2)=7,875 pair tests and 15,750 additions/subtractions. The compact
+route instead takes each of 126 home distances modulo 127 and groups equal
+residues.
 
-Inverse construction.  Sample distinct nonzero coefficients a,b with
-a != +/-b first.  Sample distinct fiber values whose sum is zero, and draw three
-distinct points on each affine line a*u+b*v=value.  Consequently the total tag
-sum is orthogonal to (a,b).  Attach the three points of each fiber to a hidden
-tree-metric center.  A true triplet perimeter has no center-to-root edge; every
